## Summary of what I could establish (and what I could not)

Caveat up front: the `rtk` tool in this workspace shells out to plain `grep` without `-r`/`-n`, so it (a) fails on directory paths and (b) **emits no line numbers**. Every "evidence" item below is therefore a *verified match in a named file*, not a verified `file:line`. I have not fabricated line numbers, and I could not run `git log`/`git blame` (no shell tool available), so I cannot confirm any upstream commit hash.

## The chain, as visible in this tree

**1. Two symlinks are created per (partner, usb device) pair** — `drivers/usb/typec/class.c`, in `typec_partner_link_device()`:

```c
ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
if (ret) return;
ret = sysfs_create_link(&partner->dev.kobj, &dev->kobj, dev_name(dev));
if (ret) { sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec"); return; }
```

So `typec` lives **on the USB device's kobject**, pointing at the partner. Teardown in `typec_partner_unlink_device()` is the mirror, and it is **unconditional** — it never checks whether the link was ever created:

```c
sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");
```

**2. The USB-device pointers are cached in the port**, `drivers/usb/typec/class.h`:

```c
struct device *usb2_dev;
struct device *usb3_dev;
struct mutex  partner_link_lock;
```

`typec_partner_attach()` (class.c) selects the slot by speed and only links if a partner already exists:

```c
struct usb_device *udev = to_usb_device(dev);
if (udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER) port->usb2_dev = dev; else port->usb3_dev = dev;
... typec_partner_link_device(partner, dev);
```

Note the asymmetry: **`usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` are recorded even when no partner exists, i.e. with no symlink created.** `typec_register_partner()` later links whatever is cached.

**3. `typec_unregister_partner()` unlinks from the cached pointers**, under `partner_link_lock`:

```c
mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);
if (port->usb2_dev) { typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev); port->usb2_dev = NULL; }
if (port->usb3_dev) { typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev); port->usb3_dev = NULL; }
mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
device_unregister(&partner->dev);
```

**4. Who feeds attach/deattach** — `drivers/usb/core/port.c` `connector_bind()` sets `port_dev->connector` and immediately calls `typec_attach(port_dev->connector, &port_dev->child->dev)`; `drivers/usb/core/hub.c` `usb_disconnect()` calls `typec_deattach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev)` and then `device_del(&udev->dev)`. The dispatch is the `con->attach`/`con->deattach` indirection in `include/linux/usb/typec.h` (`typec_attach`/`typec_deattach`), wired to `typec_partner_attach`/`typec_partner_deattach` in class.c.

**5. UCSI side** — `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c`: `ucsi_handle_connector_change()` (registered as `INIT_WORK(&con->work, ucsi_handle_connector_change)`, hence `kworker/6:0`) reacts to `UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE` / `UCSI_CONSTAT_PARTNER_CHANGE` and calls `ucsi_unregister_partner(con)`, which does `typec_set_mode(TYPEC_STATE_SAFE)` → `ucsi_unregister_partner_pdos()` → `ucsi_unregister_altmodes(SOP)` → `typec_unregister_partner(con->partner)` → `con->partner = NULL`.

## Why kernfs returns ENOENT

`kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()` in `fs/kernfs/dir.c` contains exactly the observed `WARN(1, ... "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n")`, which fires when the named child cannot be found under the parent kernfs node. Reaching it with name `"typec"` means the removal target `&dev->kobj` (a `usb_device`) either never got the `typec` symlink or already lost it. Both are reachable from the code above:

- `port->usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` can be non-NULL while **no** `typec` link exists (attach with `partner == NULL`), yet `typec_unregister_partner()` unconditionally unlinks both slots.
- `typec_deattach()` runs *before* `device_del(&udev->dev)` in `usb_disconnect()`, but only `partner_link_lock` serialises it against the UCSI work; once the usb device's kernfs dir is torn down, a still-cached pointer makes the second `sysfs_remove_link()` an ENOENT.

Because `typec_partner_unlink_device()` has no "was this linked?" state and no return-value handling, the double/spurious removal surfaces as the kernfs WARN rather than being swallowed.

## Fix status

- **In this tree:** the `partner_link_lock` mitigation *is* present (`class.h` field, `mutex_init(&port->partner_link_lock)` and locking in register/unregister/attach/deattach). So the coarse register-vs-attach race is already addressed. What is **not** present is any per-link bookkeeping or `device_is_registered()` / `kobj.state_in_sysfs` guard — I searched `drivers/usb/typec/class.c` for `device_is_registered` and `state_in_sysfs` and got **no matches**. That residual gap is consistent with the warning you see.
- **Upstream:** I cannot verify a specific commit/series from here. I deliberately will not cite a hash I haven't confirmed. To close this out you'd want `git log --oneline -- drivers/usb/typec/class.c` around the `partner_link_lock` introduction and anything touching `typec_partner_link_device`/`typec_partner_unlink_device` after it.

